"Steal It" is an horror game inspired by #link("https://store.steampowered.com/app/1349060/It_Steals")[It Steals], designed to bring a similar horror experience to head-mounted displays. Developed in Unity and using Ubiq as the underlining network infrastructure, the game consists in a group of players that need to team up together to escape a labyrinth inhabited by a bloodthirsty ghost known as "The Monster".

Specifically, after starting the game, people are automatically teleported to the game map: the goal is to reach the final room, which is locked by various lock whose keys are scattered around the labyrinth. Players need to explore the map, collect all the keys and unlock the final room's door, while at the same time escaping the ghost and opening locked areas containing keys and useful objects, like the see-through goggles, by interacting with buttons and lever, as well as climbing ladders to reach for ghost-safe areas.

In order for players to defend themselves from The Monster, but to also have a better vision, each of them is equipped with a torch they can point to The Monster to stun it and make it go away for a brief period of time. However, the power of the torch is not unlimited: players must search for special spherical batteries that allows them to charge their torch. Once inserted, players can freely steal others batteries, even if still inserted in another player torch.

Theoretically speaking, the group wanted to test whether it is true that immersion can be really created with less realism but more as a psychological state, providing the player with a continuos set of emotions and isolating it from the real environment and also creating an extended reality horror-like experience provided to the player with the developed project.

Another aspect the group wanted to analyze is whether a mixed reality experience could help led people to team up during scary situation or if they tend to break the group in order to win the game by themselves.

The team hypothesis is that a game should not only be only about realism, but presence could also be accomplish with less realism and more emotions.

As for the group personal experience, horror games can became really immersive and abstract the player from the real world even without the usage of an head-mounted display, our goal was to bring that experience to the extended reality world and see if the same level of involvement could be achieved in the virtual world even with a smaller amount of realism but with an higher amount of interaction.

A possible positive answer to the just mentioned proposition could have several consequences in the digital game development, where, specifically regarding horror games, realism was the one of the key factor: this project can reveal that this could at least dependent from the targeted platform.